==Reign==
===Accession and early years===
Bacha ascended the throne on his father's 75th birthday, on DAY MONTH {{abbr|2409|2001 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. His coronation took place on DAY MONTH, on the occasion of which he addressed his people on national television, promising BLA BLA BLA. 

===Domestic policies===

===Foreign policy===

==Ideology==
Bacha's ideology has been described as [[wikipedia:populism|populist]], with a modern and progressive attitude towards economic and administrative issues, and a traditional and conservative stance on social and political issues. Internationally, it has been described as [[wikipedia:Paternalistic conservatism|paternalistic conservatism]] and a continuation of his father and grand-father's royal legacy. 

===Economic policy===
Bacha has described his economic views as a "constant exercise in optimization to solve the problem of imperfect information in market economies," with the ultimate goal of lowering the cost of living for his subjects while simultaneously improving their quality of life. He advocates for a [[wikipedia:dirigisme|dirigist]] approach to national economics, emulating his grandfather [[Bacha XVI of Qym|Bacha XVI]]'s approach, in contrast to his father [[Bacha XVII of Qym|Bacha XVII]]'s more liberal and laissez-faire policies. 

Bacha criticized the use of subsidies and bailouts, stating "the Crown's responsibility is to the people, not corporations," further arguing that the number one priority in his reign was to fight income inequality. He has advocated for more fiscal regulation of the stock market, tax havens and tax avoidance at large. 

===Foreign policy===
Bacha summarized Qym's role within [[Basis]] as a "[[wikipedia:humanism|humanist]] [[wikipedia:great power|great power]]". He stressed the importance of human rights and improving the quality of life for all humans. He denounced [[transhumanism]] and [[fascism]], describing them as "hateful ideologies incompatible with humane societies". 

===Environment===
In his first speech as king, Bacha stressed the importance of fighting climate change and transitioning away from fossil fuels into clean energy and a clean future. (AL GORE TYPE OF STUFF I GUESS)

===Healthcare===
Bacha has advocated for more privatization of healthcare and the implementation of a mixed system of private and public hospitals. He stressed the role of the Crown to provide universal healthcare to all his subjects, but recognized the convenience of private hospitals to alleviate the public system. He has however warned against the exploitation of sick people for monetary gains and instead seeks a "balance between private and public care". 

===Education===
Bacha has warned against teachers "distorting reality" and "weaponizing their classes to bolster private agendas" such as the teachings of [[wikipedia:anti-gender movement|gender theory]]. 

===Democracy===
Bacha has ruled out a democratic system for Qym, describing democracy as "a delusion where people falsely believe they make a choice out of free will, that their vote has an impact, when the reality is the opposite." He argues polling agencies, lobbyists, the media, and pre-established political parties all influence voters, who choose according to limited and selective information, electing candidates to political offices who are more often than not, in his view, utterly incompetent. He has named [[RIVAL DEMOCRACY]] as an example of "democratic delusion," as he labels it. 

===Security===
Bacha advocates for greater security in the modern digital age, particularly regarding fraud, which often target senior citizens. He has also criticized anti-law-enforcement movements such as "[[wikipedia:defund the police|defund the police]]", describing them as "naive and misguided". 

===LGBT rights===
Bacha has made many mixed actions and statements with regard to the [[LGBT rights in Qym|Qymish LGBT community]] during his reign. At the beginning of his reign, he stated he supported "gay rights". He notably legalized same-sex [[wikipedia:unregistered cohabitation|unregistered cohabitation]] in {{abbr|2419|2008.7 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, but positioned himself against [[wikipedia:same-sex adoption|same-sex adoptions]] and what he labeled the "growing threat of [[wikipedia:Anti-gender movement|gender theory]]", claiming they "undermine family values" and that a child "needs a mom and a dad, not only two dads or only two moms". He further added that there are "only two genders, men and women" and that Qymish society was accommodating of LGBT people, but that "there are limits" and that the Crown will allow what is "common sense", which he clarified as being [[wikipedia:cisgenderism|gender binarism]]. 

In {{abbr|2436|2024 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Bacha shrugged and asked if it was a bad thing, after a TV anchor asked him what his thoughts were on being characterized as a [[wikipedia:transphobia|transphobe]] and [[wikipedia:heterosexism|heterosexist]] by LGBT advocacy groups. He added: "If one of my subjects wants to pretend to be the opposite gender, they can, it's their right to be delusional, but normal people also have the right not to entertain that delusion. If I were to wear a wig, a dress and some makeup, I may like how I look, but most people would think I lost my mind". Asked, whether he qualified non-binary people as mentally ill, he confirmed that was the case. "What is it other than mental illness? They call it the [[wikipedia:gender dysphoria|gender identity disorder]] for a reason." Bacha then dismissed a correction that [[wikipedia:ICD-11|ICD-11]] uses the term [[wikipedia:gender incongruence|gender incongruence]] and does not consider it a mental illness, saying "they are fighting with semantics to justify something that lacks any common sense to you, me, and everyday people".

==Public image==
Bacha has faced heavy media scrutiny since his birth, which increased dramatically upon reaching adulthood and being the heir apparent to his father, [[Bacha XVII of Qym|Bacha XVII]]. His relationship with this wife-to-be [[Queen Qümica of Qym|Qümica]] particularly drew media speculation and misinformation, as well as his relationship with his children, specifically his eldest son [[Mirmo ish Zhifol|Mirmo]] and in later years, his second son and heir apparent [[Prince Vuqac of Qym|Vuqac]]. However, Bacha's public image has been relatively constant since his childhood as that of a down-to-earth royal who dresses modestly and connects easily with his subjects. 

[[NEWSPAPER]] noted the jump in public perceptions from different eras. His grandfather [[Bacha XVI of Qym|Bacha XVI]]'s reign, the [[Sixteenth Bachan era]], was impassive and distant, with the general public rarely seeing or hearing from him. In contrast, his father [[Bacha XVII of Qym|Bacha XVII]]'s reign, the [[Seventeenth Bachan era]], was characterized by constant television appearances and a celebrity-like following. Bacha's reign, dubbed the [[Eighteenth Bachan era]], was a mix of both, with regular television appearances, but a marked shift away from exposing the core royal family's private affairs. Bacha only allowed questions regarding his reign and never answered personal questions about his family. Royal expert and marketing strategist [[Milas Terin]] summarized Bacha's public persona as a "well calculated apparatus, combining his highly-educated royal eloquence with relatable references and attire, making him seem as above everyone from an intellectual standpoint, reminding us of his regal superiority, while also showcasing an amiable behavioral juxtaposition, making him seem approachable nonetheless." 

Terin describes Bacha as an exceptional orator and diplomat who, according to him, must have had media and speech training from a young age. Throughout his reign, Bacha delivered a series of monthly video addresses on social media platforms, informing the public directly of his decisions. He also appeared on numerous live streams and podcasts later in his reign to better connect with the newer generations. 

===Opinion polls===
Throughout his reign as king, Bacha has maintained high public approval ratings, consistently polling above 67%. 

Demographically, Bacha's strongest support has been recorded among adults above {{abbr|60|50 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, with approval ranging from 70% to as high as 90% at times. Support has been lower among the Qymish youth, with approval among those aged {{abbr|20|17 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}-{{abbr|30|25 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} polling between 45% and 55%. 

